[38;5;231m⏺[39m [38;5;153mindex.html:13[39m loads [38;5;153mapp.js[39m as a classic script — no [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m, no bundler — which will constrain how the store is
  shared. I'll deal with that in the design; it's not a question for you.

  [1mThe fork:[0m "persist" has three different lifetimes, and this one is genuinely hard to change later because it's a
  stored format — once other forms read the store, changing where it lives means a migration path for users mid-session.

  - [1mIn-memory only[0m — a variable in a shared scope. Zero storage risk, nothing to clean up. But it's lost on every page
    load, so if your other forms are separate HTML pages, the user is "not logged in" the moment they navigate. Only
    viable if the whole app is one page.
  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — survives reload and same-tab navigation, cleared when the tab closes. Matches what a login session
    normally means, and it's per-tab, so two tabs can't clobber each other. Doesn't survive a browser restart.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives browser restart, so the user stays "logged in" indefinitely until explicitly cleared. Shared
    across tabs. On a shared machine, the previous user's ID is sitting there for the next person, and stale IDs are
    the usual source of "wrong user" bugs.

  [1mMy recommendation: [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m.[0m A login result should last as long as the browsing session and no longer, and
  per-tab isolation avoids the multi-tab clobbering that [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m invites. It's a user ID rather than a credential,
  so the exposure is identity info rather than account access — but that's still worth not leaving on a shared machine
  after the tab closes.
